<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manco Inca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1515–<a href="/years/1544">1544</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Manco Inca Yupanqui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manco <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> installed as Inca ruler in 1533. Ill-treated by the Spanish, he rebelled in 1536 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> for months, and won a battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ollantaytambo">Ollantaytambo</a> in 1537. Failing to retake the city, he withdrew to Vilcabamba in the forested mountains, founding the Neo-Inca State. He was killed in 1544 by Spanish fugitives he had sheltered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manco-inca.jpg" alt="Manco Inca" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SAHUARAURA_p108_MANCO_INCA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manco <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>, also called Manco II, was one of more than fifty sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> and a younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>. He was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> around 1515, and his early history is obscure. He took part in the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisuyu">Antisuyu</a> in the years 1532 to 1533. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa</a>'s troops took Cuzco under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quizquiz">Quizquiz</a> they killed Huayna Capac's descendants, the supporters of Huáscar and anyone who might replace the Inca, so Manco fled and avoided the Atahualpists. On 14 November 1533 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>. That meeting and events such as the killing of Atahualpa led him to believe that the Spaniards were saviours sent by the gods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the death of Túpac Huallpa, a puppet ruler crowned by Pizarro, Manco joined Pizarro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cajamarca">Cajamarca</a>. When Pizarro's forces reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> he had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a> acknowledge Manco as their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a>. Pizarro then left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jauja">Jauja</a> in pursuit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quizquiz">Quizquiz</a>, leaving his younger brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a> and Juan Pizarro as regidores with a garrison of ninety men. The brothers so mistreated Manco that he tried to escape in 1535, failed and was imprisoned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-pizarro">Hernando Pizarro</a> released him to recover a golden statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>, and he escaped a second time.</p><p>Taking advantage of Almagro's absence, Manco gathered an army, put at 200,000 warriors, and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a> in early 1536, though factions such as Paullu Inca's would not join. A siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> was led by his captain Quiso Yupanqui, and the Incas defeated four relief expeditions sent by Pizarro, in which nearly 500 Spanish soldiers died. After ten months Manco withdrew to the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ollantaytambo">Ollantaytambo</a>, where in January 1537 he successfully defended against Spanish attacks. He then gave up the highlands, retreating to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitcos">Vitcos</a> and finally to Vilcabamba, where he founded the Neo-Inca State, which lasted until the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a> in 1572.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Spaniards crowned Manco's younger half-brother Paullu Inca as a puppet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a>. They captured Manco's sister-wife Cura Ocllo, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a> raped, and had her murdered in 1539. After many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> battles in the mountains of Vilcabamba, Manco was murdered in 1544 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitcos">Vitcos</a> by supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a>, who had assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and were hiding under his protection. Manco's soldiers killed them in turn. His body was preserved at Vilcabamba in a Temple of the Sun, and his son Sayri Tupaq succeeded him. His other sons included Titu Cusi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manco Inca Yupanqui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the help of Andean peoples hostile to the Incas, Pizarro entered Cusco in November 1533 and installed <b>Manco Inca</b> as a puppet ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire, Francisco Pizarro executed Atahualpa in 1533, entered Cusco and installed <b>Manco Inca</b>, a son of Huayna Capac, as a puppet ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-inca-state">Neo-Inca State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Returning from Chile in 1537, he seized Cusco from Hernando and Gonzalo Pizarro during the rebellion of <b>Manco Inca</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-new-toledo">Governorate of New Toledo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in Vilcabamba, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.52,-71.98&amp;year=1533" aria-label="Cusco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2577 2667 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Manco Inca"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3027 2937h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.52,-71.98&amp;year=1533">What was here in 1533? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
